{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F18Float32CollapsesIngestedKeys",
    "description": "FINDING F18 (expected to FAIL). A document-wide domain.element_type: \"Float32\" rounds INGESTED values to binary32 too, so an integer key above 2^24 = 16777216 is silently corrupted and distinct keys collide.\n\nMeasured on the real snapshot table, this document read under each precision:\n    Float64: 1183 rows, 214 distinct SCC, first 2260001010\n    Float32: 1183 rows,  48 distinct SCC, first 2260001024\n\n214 equipment categories collapse to 48. MOVES SCC codes are ten digits (~2.26e9), 135x above the limit; 2265007010 and 2265007015 both become 2265007104, so a join.on over them merges two different categories.\n\nEnd to end on fixtures/nr-logging-county.esm, adding nothing but the domain block:\n    binary64: SCC 2260007005, emissionQuant 1.0997e-07 .. 1205.1395\n    Float32 : SCC 2260006912, emissionQuant ALL EXACTLY ZERO\n\nThe document validates and the run completes.\n\nNOT the const path: a const array of the same three codes round-trips exactly under Float32. The corruption is on the ingested/float_columns path, which is where a relational model keys live. EarthSciAST 973ee7360 already refuses a declared index-set EXTENT above 2^24 because index arithmetic shares the value kernels; the gate does not cover data columns.\n\nWhy this is a design question, not a patch. Honouring Float32 was meant to reproduce the reference real*4 arithmetic (PLAN.md 1.6.2). But the reference is f32 in its FLOATING-POINT quantities while its keys stay Fortran INTEGER and CHARACTER, never REAL*4. A document-wide float precision cannot express that split: it reproduces the arithmetic and destroys the keys. Either key/integer columns are exempt from the document precision -- a typed-column notion the format lacks -- or precision becomes per-expression."
  },
  "domain": {
    "element_type": "Float32"
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "n": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "r": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "n"
    }
  },
  "data_sources": {
    "S": {
      "kind": "points",
      "metadata": {
        "esio_format": "parquet"
      },
      "source": {
        "url_template": "file://${MOVES_SNAPSHOTS}/nr-logging-county/tables/db__movesexecution1ccc0232_campuscluster_illinois_edu__nrsourceusetype.parquet"
      },
      "reader_options": {
        "float_columns": [
          "SCC"
        ]
      },
      "extent": {
        "metaparameter": "n"
      }
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "M": {
      "variables": {
        "scc": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "r"
          ],
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "S",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "SCC"
            }
          }
        },
        "echoed": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "r"
          ]
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "lhs": "echoed",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "i"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "i": {
                "from": "r"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "index",
              "args": [
                "scc",
                "i"
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "an_ingested_integer_key_survives_the_document_precision",
          "description": "nrsourceusetype's first SCC is 2260001010. Under Float64 it reads back exactly; under Float32 it becomes 2260001024, and the column's 214 distinct codes collapse to 48. A key that does not round-trip is a key a join cannot match on. Asserted at ZERO tolerance deliberately: the default 1e-6 relative tolerance CANNOT see this. |2260001024 - 2260001010| / 2.26e9 is 6.2e-09, four orders inside it -- the corruption is relatively tiny and semantically total, which is exactly why a key set must be compared exactly and never within a tolerance.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "echoed",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 2260001010,
              "coords": {
                "r": 1
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 0.0,
                "abs": 0.0
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}